  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A sprawling area of high pressure will reside over \nmuch of the Eastern U.S. through the upcoming week. Days will be\nwarming each day, but overnight lows will continue below normal\nwith freezing temps possible each morning through mid week. \nBlended guidance struggles to capture lows in good radiational \nsetups like this, and lows were dropped below blended guidance \nthrough mid week to account for this. The coldest nights look \nto be Monday night and Tuesday night. Despite many areas seeing \nfrost/freeze impacts, the program has been ended for much of ENC\ndue to the widespread freeze that occurred Sunday morning. The\nexception is Downeast Carteret, Pamlico, Hyde, and Outer Banks \nDare counties. With this update, Frost Advisories are in effect\nfor Downeast Carteret, Mainland Hyde, and Pamlico counties where\nconfidence is highest on seeing temps below 36F tonight. The\nfrost advisory is from 12AM to 8 AM. Additional Frost/Freeze\nheadlines may be needed Tuesday night/Wednesday morning.\n\nLow-level thicknesses will steadily warm mid to late week, and even \nthough high pressure will remain in place, temps will warm to\nabove climo with 70s inland. For the OBX, highs will be \ntempered by light northeast breezes off the cooler waters of \nthe Labrador Current as water temps have cooled to the upper\n50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A very dry airmass remaining in place over the \ncoming week will exacerbate the worsening drought conditions across \nENC. Fortunately winds are not expected to be strong with the high \nnearby. However, fire weather concerns may grow with the curing \nvegetation combined with low RH values each afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...There is potential for a coastal low to gain \nstrength off the FL coast late in the week. While there is much \nuncertainty on where the system tracks, the last several model \ncycles of the AIFS, GFS and GFS AI have indicated a \nnortheastward track off the Southeast US coast. AI guidance and\nthe CMC continue to show a track closer to eastern NC, while the\nGEFS and EPS members show a more muted low further offshore. We\nwill continue to monitor the system, as large waves and\nassociated coastal impacts are becoming more likely along the\nOuter Banks.",
